		<title><![CDATA[5 mistakes beginners make in their first month at the gym]]></title>
		<link>http://localhost:8080/5-mistakes-beginners-make-in-their-first-month-at-the-gym/</link>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:58:44 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[ironpeak-admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://localhost:8080/5-mistakes-beginners-make-in-their-first-month-at-the-gym/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Your first month sets the tone for everything after it. These are the five mistakes our coaches see most often, and the simple fix for each.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">1. Skipping the warm-up
Five minutes of light cardio and a few practice sets with an empty bar prepare your joints and make your working sets feel easier.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">2. Copying someone else&#039;s program
The advanced lifter next to you trains for a different goal. Start with a simple full-body plan three days a week and add volume slowly.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">3. Going too heavy too soon
Ego lifting is the fastest route to an injury. Pick a weight you can lift for every rep with good form, then add a little each week.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">4. Doing only cardio
Treadmill time is useful, but strength training is what changes your shape and protects your joints. Aim for both.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">5. Not asking for help
Every coach on our floor is there to answer questions. Ask them to check your squat or deadlift form. That is what they are for.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Skipping the warm-up, copying someone else's program and lifting too heavy too soon. Here is how to avoid the mistakes our coaches see most often.]]></excerpt:encoded>
