					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[What to eat before a morning workout]]></title>
		<link>http://localhost:8080/what-to-eat-before-a-morning-workout/</link>
		<pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 03:58:45 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[ironpeak-admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://localhost:8080/what-to-eat-before-a-morning-workout/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Training first thing in the morning is a great habit, but an empty tank can leave you dizzy halfway through your session. A small snack 30 to 60 minutes before usually solves it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick options that work
A banana with a spoon of peanut butter. A small bowl of poha. Two slices of toast with curd. A handful of soaked almonds with a glass of milk. Keep it light and easy to digest.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What to avoid
Heavy, oily or very high-fibre food right before training can upset your stomach. Save the parathas for after your workout.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">After training
Within an hour or two, eat a proper meal with protein and carbohydrates, such as eggs with roti, dal with rice, or paneer with vegetables. This is when your muscles recover and grow.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Training at 6 am on an empty stomach? A small, easy-to-digest snack 30 to 60 minutes before can make a big difference to your energy.]]></excerpt:encoded>
