					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[How many days a week should you train?]]></title>
		<link>http://localhost:8080/how-many-days-a-week-should-you-train/</link>
		<pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 03:58:46 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[ironpeak-admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://localhost:8080/how-many-days-a-week-should-you-train/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The honest answer is: as many days as you can keep doing for months, not weeks. Consistency beats intensity every time.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Beginners: 3 days
Three full-body sessions a week, with a rest day between each, is enough to build strength and learn the main lifts properly.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Intermediate: 4 to 5 days
Once the basics feel solid, an upper/lower split or a push/pull/legs routine lets you train each muscle twice a week with more volume.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Listen to recovery
If you sleep badly, feel sore for days or your lifts keep going down, you need more rest, not more training. Rest days are when progress actually happens.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[More is not always better. The right number of training days depends on your goal, your recovery and how consistent you can be.]]></excerpt:encoded>
